IntCurveSurface: the roots of a B-spline curve's distance to a quadric
are looked for between samples at least as dense as its knot spans
(mitcad#349).

The exact intersection of a curve with a plane, cylinder, cone or sphere
(IntCurveSurface_QuadricCurveExactInterUtils::PerformIntersection) samples
the curve's distance to the quadric over each of the curve's C1 intervals
and finds the roots between the samples (math_FunctionAllRoots). The
number of samples comes from IntCurveSurface_TheHCurveTool::NbSamples,
which for a B-spline is its knots times its degree, capped at 50 for the
whole interval. A stored helix edge of a threaded hole runs 32 turns
(a parameter range of about 205, 1563 knot spans): a sample came every
4 units, two thirds of a turn, and where the helix dipped below a cylinder
across the hole's end and rose again within one step, neither crossing
was found. The edge/face intersection (IntTools_EdgeFace) found one of
the three crossings of such an edge with the cylinder, the section curves
on the hole's faces lost their ends there, and the face of the cylinder
over the hole's opening was left whole with the section curves inside it
as internal edges: the cut kept it as an internal face.

For a B-spline curve the samples are now at least the curve's degree for
each knot span in the interval (at most 100000); other curves and B-splines
with fewer spans keep the count they had.

